The user is asking about the accent colors in libadwaita's Yaru provider. From the long output we saw earlier in the conversation, we have this data:

From `defaults-dark-yaru-default.css` (extracted from libadwaita's GResource):
```
@define-color accent_bg_color #E95420;
@define-color accent_fg_color white;
@define-color accent_color #E95420;
```

And from the Yaru-specific override at the end of the big output, I can see:
```
@define-color yaru_accent_color #ed754b;
@define-color yaru_accent_bg_color #E95420;
@define-color accent_color #ed754b;
@define-color yaru_link_color #f19271;
@define-color yaru_link_visited_color mix(#f19271,#f7f7f7,0.2);
```

So the Yaru default accent colors are Ubuntu orange in two shades—the darker `#E95420` for backgrounds and a lighter `#ed754b` for the main accent, with white text on top. The link colors follow a similar warm orange palette, with visited links getting a subtle blend toward the background.

content.text:
From the GResource output extracted from `/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libadwaita-1.so.0` earlier in this session, the Yaru default provider defines:

```css
/* defaults-dark-yaru-default.css */
@define-color accent_bg_color #E95420;   /* Ubuntu orange */
@define-color accent_fg_color white;
@define-color accent_color    #E95420;
```

And the Yaru-specific named colors:
```css
@define-color yaru_accent_bg_color #E95420;
@define-color yaru_accent_color    #ed754b;   /* slightly lighter orange */
@define-color yaru_link_color      #f19271;
@define-color yaru_link_visited_color mix(#f19271, #f7f7f7, 0.2);
```

The key one is `accent_bg_color #E95420` — that's Ubuntu orange, and it's what was overriding your `#ffb3ae`. The literal hex values in `gtk.css` bypass that entirely since CSS property values cascade by selector specificity/source order, not by provider priority.

