`gtk.css` is now clean. Here's the final state of all three files:

**`gtk.css`**
```css
@import "colors.css";

window, .background { background-color: @window_bg_color; color: @window_fg_color; }
headerbar { background-color: @headerbar_bg_color; color: @headerbar_fg_color; }
.view, scrolledwindow > viewport, textview, textview > text {
  background-color: @view_bg_color; color: @view_fg_color;
}
```

**`colors.css` — surface depth layer:**
| Surface | Color |
|---|---|
| Window (base) | `#131313` |
| View/content | `#1a1a1a` |
| Headerbar | `#1f1f1f` |
| Card / thumbnail / secondary sidebar | `#252525` |
| Popover / dialog | `#2a2a2a` |

**`settings.ini`** — `gtk-theme-name=Yaru-purple` (adw-gtk3 for GTK3, purple accent for GTK4)

The `colorreload-gtk-module` in your loaded modules will keep mutating `gtk.css` when apps reload styles. You may want to keep an eye on that — it's the thing creating the duplicate blocks.

